{
  "id": "ilford-sfx200",
  "label": "ILFORD SFX 200",
  "role": "negative_bw",
  "balance": "daylight",
  "source": "HARMAN technology Limited, ILFORD SFX 200 Technical Information (Nov 2018); curves traced from the PDF's raster chart images (spectral page 1, characteristic curve page 2)",
  "notes": "Approximate digitization; not supplied or endorsed by HARMAN technology. Both charts are embedded raster images (no vector curve paths); traced at the images' native resolution after thin grid and frame lines were removed by a 3x3 grey opening, axes calibrated on the printed grid lines and tick marks. Characteristic curve (page 2): the datasheet prints a single curve (ILFORD ID-11 stock, 10 min at 20 C/68 F, intermittent agitation); all_curves holds only this curve. x is the chart's own 'Relative log exposure' axis (labels 1-4, arbitrary origin), not log H in lux-seconds; density axis labelled 1.0 and 2.0, zero at the bottom grid line by the equal grid spacing; tracing noise in the flat toe was removed with a running maximum so density is non-decreasing. log_h_ref: none. The chart carries no 18% grey, speed-point or normal-exposure mark; the text gives ISO 200/24 to daylight measured with ILFORD ID-11 at 20 C/68 F (page 1). The model's own black-and-white speed-point rule places mid grey; no grey point is set from this datasheet or interpreted here. Spectral sensitivity (page 1): 'Wedge spectrogram to tungsten light (2850K)'; the chart draws 'Conventional film' and 'SFX 200' on one axis and only the SFX 200 curve is traced (column tracking past the crossing near 637 nm, the label pointer columns skipped); the vertical axis is labelled 'Sensitivity' with ticks 0.5 and 1.0 only and no statement whether it is linear or logarithmic; values are read on that tick scale as charted, the chart's bottom frame lies at -0.075 on it, so the end points read slightly below zero. VISIBLE BAND ONLY: the chart runs 353-773 nm and the datasheet states extended red sensitivity up to 740 nm, but camera RGB carries no near-infrared signal, so spectral_sensitivity is cut at 700 nm (value 0.790 there) and the 701-773 nm part of the curve is not used; the near-infrared response is not modelled or inferred, and the extended-red look (dark skies, light foliage behind a deep red filter) is not reproduced by this profile. No filter is applied (the datasheet's filter factors are not modelled). No granularity, MTF or contrast-time data are published; grain uses the project fallback.",
